excess deaths for people over 65 reached  the highest level since records began  during this Summer's heat waves a joint  report by the UK Health Security Agency  and the office for National statistics  shows the effect of this Summer's record  temperatures on excess mortality well  Skies correspondent Matthew Thompson has  all the details for us now a morning to  you Matthew what was the size of this  effect from the heat wave well fairly  substantial I mean we've known for some  time that heat waves can be deadly  particularly for the elderly but these  figures really illustrate quite how  deadly they can be and there's actually  two sets of figures out today from the  UK HSA and the ons and I'll take them  each by turn the first is specifically  by the UK HSA health Security Agency and  it looked at excess deaths in each of  the specific five Heat Wave periods over  the summer in England amongst the  elderly and they make for some fairly  Grim reading so across all five heat  waves from June to August there were  2803 excess deaths among the over 65s  that's defined as deaths above the  average levels just before and after the  heat wave struck and it's actually the  highest figure since records began in  2004 and during the hottest period which  was the 17th to the 20th of July that's  when temperatures you'll remember hit 40  degrees there were  1012 excess deaths among the over 65s  but the most deadly period was from the  8th to the 17th of August it was less  hot then but it was longer lasting and  that saw  1458 excess deaths in those over 65. and  then we have the ons data which looks  more broadly at the time period from  June to August and it showed that there  are 3  271 excess deaths for all age groups now  that's clearly a higher number than the  UK HSA figure but as I say it includes  all age groups and unlike the other data  it doesn't exclude deaths from covert  during that period so perhaps the UK HSA  figure is a more accurate reading of the  impact of the heat at least on elderly  deaths but either way it's fairly clear  that heat can kill and that Britain is  perhaps uniquely ill-equipped to deal  with the kinds of temperatures that we  may have to get used to in the years  ahead and that's going to require fairly  urgent action from the government on  things like retrofitting houses ensuring  there are shutters better ventilation  more green spaces and urban environments  for example to ensure that excess death  like these don't become more commonplace  in the years ahead
 

